      limitations = "THE SOURCE FOR BENAZEPRIL AND THE FULL TEXT IS NEEDED; only the abstract has been read (2026-10-20, from a PubMed screen of the 25 benazepril/benazeprilat pharmacokinetic hits, in which the Ciba-Geigy series of Kaiser, Waldmeier and Sioufi is the whole primary literature and everything else is a special-population comparison, an interaction study or a review). The abstract gives benazepril tmax 0.5 h and half-life 0.6 h with only trace amounts excreted unchanged, and benazeprilat tmax 1.5 h with BIPHASIC elimination: about 80 percent of what is formed goes within 24 h at a half-life of 2.7 h, with a terminal phase of 22.3 h carrying a minor amount, 17 percent of the dose recovered in 24-h urine as benazeprilat, an AUC accumulation ratio of 1.20 and an effective accumulation half-life of 10-11 h. Two further papers give what would be the external validation: Sun 1994 (PMID 7867683) prints benazepril AUC(0-4 h) 140 ng.h/mL, Cmax 149 ng/mL, tmax 0.6 h and benazeprilat AUC(0-24 h) 1410 ng.h/mL, Cmax 257 ng/mL, tmax 1.5 h after the same 10 mg dose, and Waldmeier 1991 (PMID 2058180) is a 14C mass balance putting absorption at 37 percent of the dose with benazeprilat about half the plasma radioactivity and glucuronides of both moieties another 33 percent. THE PARENT IS DETERMINED FROM THE ABSTRACTS AND THE METABOLITE IS NOT. One compartment for benazepril closes on Sun\'s AUC and the reported half-life (CL/F about 71 L/h, V/F about 62 L, and a ka near 2.7 /h from tmax 0.55 h against k 1.155 /h, arithmetic done here). For benazeprilat, AUC = fm x Dose / CL gives one equation in two unknowns, and the urinary 17 percent does NOT pin the formation fraction, because Waldmeier shows a third of the radioactivity leaving as glucuronides and the class review (Kelly 1990, PMID 2203579) reports biliary elimination for benazeprilat specifically. A STRUCTURAL CAUTION FOR WHOEVER BUILDS IT: benazeprilat\'s 22.3 h terminal phase carrying only a minor amount of the elimination is the signature of saturable binding to converting enzyme, the same phenomenon the enalaprilat record flags, so a two-compartment LINEAR fit to that tail would be the wrong structure rather than an approximation of the right one -- which is exactly why captopril was dropped. Kaiser 1989\'s full text, with its fitted parameters over 62 subjects, is what would say whether the published fit avoids that or inherits it."),
  rec("losartan__iv_and_oral__oral__healthy_men__concentration__lo1995",
      "losartan", "losartan and EXP3174", "oral",
      "intravenous solution and immediate-release tablet",
      population = "18 healthy men given intravenous losartan, intravenous EXP3174 and oral losartan in a crossover",
      endpoint = "concentration", analyte = "losartan and EXP3174",
      matrix = "plasma", pk_evidence = PART, pd_evidence = PRO,
      execution_status = EXTR, source_type = "primary_fitted",
      source_url = "https://doi.org/10.1016/0009-9236(95)90020-9",
      limitations = "THIS IS THE RIGHT SOURCE FOR LOSARTAN and the full text is needed; only the abstract has been read (2026-10-20, found by a PubMed screen of 52 losartan population-pharmacokinetic hits and 122 losartan/EXP3174 hits, none of which is a population model with a printed parameter table -- so this is not one source among many, it is the one study that identifies the system). Its design is what makes it decisive: separate INTRAVENOUS arms for losartan AND for EXP3174, plus oral losartan, so both moieties have ABSOLUTE clearances and volumes rather than apparent ones, and the 14 percent conversion fraction it reports closes the parent-to-metabolite link that no oral-only study can. The abstract gives losartan CL 610 mL/min, V 34 L, renal CL 70 mL/min (12 percent), terminal half-life 2.1 h; EXP3174 CL 47 mL/min, V 10 L, renal CL 26 mL/min (55 percent), terminal half-life 6.3 h; oral tmax 1 h for losartan and 3.5 h for EXP3174, AUC of the metabolite about 4x the parent, oral bioavailability 33 percent, conversion 14 percent. WHY THE ABSTRACT IS NOT ENOUGH: neither moiety is one-compartment on these numbers. Losartan\'s CL over V gives a half-life of 0.64 h against the reported 2.1 h, and EXP3174\'s gives 2.46 h against the reported 6.3 h, so the quoted volumes are steady-state or terminal volumes of a multi-compartment fit and the abstract supplies three numbers per moiety where two compartments need four -- the same arithmetic that rules out esmolol\'s and fosinopril\'s abstracts (docs/antihypertensives.md section 4). The full text\'s compartmental tables would settle it. Also needed from the full text: whether the oral conversion fraction differs from the intravenous one, which is what first-pass metabolism to EXP3174 would imply."),
  rec("enalapril__ir_tablet__oral__healthy_adults__concentration_and_urine__faisal2019",
      "enalapril", "enalapril and enalaprilat", "oral",
      "immediate-release tablet",
      population = "24 healthy adults (mean age 28 y, mean weight 69.8 kg) given a single 10 mg oral dose of enalapril maleate as the reference tablet (Renitec) and as orodispersible mini-tablets in a crossover; 2208 serum and urine concentrations of both moieties to 48 h",
      endpoint = "concentration", analyte = "enalapril and enalaprilat",
      matrix = "plasma and urine", pk_evidence = FULL, pd_evidence = PRO,
      execution_status = EXTR, source_type = "primary_fitted",
      source_url = "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2019.00973",
      limitations = "COMPLETE AND VERIFIED, and blocked on an ENGINE rather than on evidence. Table 1 read in full on 2026-10-20: KA 6.010 /h, VC 51.10 L, F1 0.606, MTT1 0.558 h with EIGHT Erlang transit compartments, KREN 0.305 /h, KM 0.688 /h (so the parent\'s elimination SPLITS into a renal and a metabolic path), and for enalaprilat VM 46.10 L, KQ1 0.060 /h, KQ2 0.054 /h, KME 0.184 /h and MTT2 0.910 h with TWO transit compartments delaying its formation. Because serum and urine were fitted together for both moieties the system is identifiable and VC, VM and the clearances are ABSOLUTE, not apparent -- F1 is a separate estimated parameter at 0.606, matching the literature\'s 60 percent. WHY IT IS NOT YET IMPLEMENTED: a chain of N IDENTICAL transit compartments has an eigenvalue repeated N times, and the library\'s closed-form coefficients all divide by differences of rate constants, which separateEigenvalues() keeps apart with a relative nudge of 1e-8. For N = 8 the partial-fraction coefficients would scale as 1/nudge^7, which is the catastrophic cancellation of nearly equal large numbers that section 3.3 rejected esmolol for -- so the nudge is not merely inelegant here, it is numerically fatal. The system IS superposable (it is linear throughout), so by the library\'s own test it does not need a numerical solver; it needs exact closed-form handling of a repeated eigenvalue, for which a MATRIX EXPONENTIAL advance over the 13-state system is the right form, and tests/testthat/test-compartments.R already carries a dependency-free scaling-and-squaring expmTaylor() that shows one costs no new package. The metabolite\'s own 2-transit delay is what forces the engine: convolution commutes, so the parent\'s input shape could have been DISCRETISED into lagged fractional doses as hydrochlorothiazide\'s Weibull was, but the parent and the metabolite would then need different discretised input sets from one dose table, which the architecture does not allow. THE PAPER\'S TRANSIT PARAMETERISATION IS INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT and it does not matter: it quotes MTT1 = (N+1)/KTR, which assumes every step shares one rate, while also defining KA as the separate rate out of the last transit. Four readings were integrated numerically by Runge-Kutta on 2026-10-20 (9 compartments at 9/MTT1 then KA; 8 with the 8th at KA; 8 at 8/MTT1; and KTR solved so the true mean input time equals MTT1) and ALL FOUR reproduce the paper\'s own reported observations -- parent tmax 0.82-0.95 h against its \'almost 1 h\', metabolite tmax 3.26-3.68 h against its \'around 3 to 4 h\' -- with Cmax varying under 5 percent. That is the opposite of the Yang 2022 valsartan case, where no reading worked, and it means the choice of reading is a documented rounding decision rather than a blocker. NOT TO BE APPLIED when it is built: the formulation covariate THETA(X) 0.730, whose target is ambiguous (the weight exponent on VC is stated to have been FIXED at 1, so 0.730 should be the formulation effect on MTT1, but read that way it moves absorption 9 minutes earlier where the text says about 5) and which in any case only describes the orodispersible mini-tablet, a formulation this library has no reason to offer; the authors themselves call both covariates clinically unimportant, the base and full models differing by under 25 percent."),
  rec("clonidine__ir_tablet__oral__normotensive_adults__bp_nonmonotone__davies1977",
